# Line endings.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS: biome.json sets `lineEnding: "lf"`, but Git on Windows
# defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites every file to CRLF on checkout.
# The result was that `pnpm lint` failed on EVERY source file on a Windows
# machine — including files nobody had touched — while CI on Linux passed
# cleanly. That is the worst kind of failure: it trains people to ignore lint.
#
# Declaring eol=lf here makes the working tree match what Biome and CI expect,
# on every OS, regardless of each developer's autocrlf setting. Git still stores
# LF internally either way; this only controls what lands on disk.
#
# After adding this, run once:  git add --renormalize .
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts must keep CRLF — cmd.exe mis-parses LF-only batch files
# (a trailing label or `goto` can silently fail).
*.bat  text eol=crlf
*.cmd  text eol=crlf
*.ps1  text eol=crlf

# Binary: never touch these, and never try to diff them as text.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.mp4  binary
*.zip  binary
